Coincidentally, one of my tasks this week
is rebuilding two Alphas. Like yourself, I'm
much more comfortable with Slackware than Redhat.
I decided I had two options: Install an old
Slackware and update, or install Redhat and try
to make it a bit more like Slackware.

1- I did find Slackware 7.1-current on cc.gatech.edu.
   Google "slackware alpha" and you'll see the link.
   You didn't say what kind of alphas you have, but
   here there are bootdisks for miata, avanti, lx164,
   and generic. I can confirm the install went ok on
   my miata without hassle; haven't tried the avanti yet.

   I wouldn't be concerned with the lagging version
   number or date. Once you have the system up and running
   you should be able to upgrade/compile what you need painles
   One caveat: you may want a Redhat CD around; I didn't
   see the Tru64 compat package or the ccc - both of
   which were installed by RedHat 7.2 .

   I haven't begun to update my system, but when I do,
   I'll throw some packages up on a website.

2- You could install Redhat and make it more Slackware-ish.
   I have copied over the inittab and /etc/rc.d files from
   a slack box to a redhat box. You'll need to doublecheck
   the paths in the scripts, but it's doable.
   I never bothered to copy the package tools over, but AFAIK
   they're just shell scripts that read /var/log/packages.
   You ought to be able to do it.

Good luck!


