Erase its original partition table first.  Otherwise installer will keep
the table type, it will not write a boot block, and you will end up with
a box bootable from a floppy or CD but not from your hard disk.

If you start an installer then you can use for that task a shell which
in some moment will be available on an alternate console.  Then you
can use dd for erasing (something like 'dd count=2 < /dev/zero > /dev/hda').
After that restart an installation from scratch.  There is a number
of other methods to the same goal.

  Michal


