Hey folks,
    Forgive me if this is a naive question. I am attempting to compile
a tweaked MySQL MAX RPMs on RedHat 7.0 on an Alpha EV56 using the ccc
compiler from Compaq. I seem to be having problems about 60 minutes into
the process. I get messages like:

target pattern contains no `%'. Stop.

With a reference to various .P files. I am not sure what these .P files 
do.  They seem to be interpreted as makefiles, and I suspect they 
relate to the linking process. But I haven't located 
any information about them.

These files seem to be generated during the build process. I am not sure
how. But things seem to choke on lines such as the following:

bind.o:	bind.c :
bind.o:	./../config.h :
bind.o:	/usr/lib/compaq/ccc-6.5.6.002-1/alpha-linux/include/stdio.h :
bind.o:	 :
/usr/include/stdio.h :
 
Looking around a bit, I found out that this was due to a 'malformed
static pattern rule'. But I think there is not supposed to be a pattern 
here. I think that the second colon is causing the problem.

I think the section above should look like this:

bind.o: bind.c 
bind.o: ./../config.h 
bind.o: /usr/lib/compaq/ccc-6.5.6.002-1/alpha-linux/include/stdio.h 
bind.o: /usr/include/stdio.h 

So the question is anybody know where these .P files come from ? If I
could figure that out, I might be able to track down the problem.

Thanks for any help you can give!

-Jonathan



