You only have to erase the disk if NT or Unix was on it, as the old dos
partition or Unix disklable could confuse the redhat install. With dos
partition, it thinks the disk is all set, installs but aboot gets written
over part the fat partition table and the disk becomes unbootable. with a
Unix disklable, the "c" partition is the entire disk.. however, if your
aware of the disklable and manipulate it, you can dual install Linux and
Tru64 Unix :^)

The latest install asks if you want to erase the existing partitions, you
should if you are making a clean install.

JJ

