Wolfgang Rupp <rupp@coredump.at> wrote:
> Lou Solomon <lny98@yahoo.com> wrote:
>> I was lucky enough to get an Alpha 4100, 4 CPU, 1GB Ram, Lots of Disk space
> Congratulations!
> 
>> 1. Purchased the Red Hat 7.1 for Alpha.  The RH seems to install perfectly,
>> but when I try to boot the install, it (SRM Loader) says "no boot block
>> written to disk".
> 
> You must use BSD disk labels. Don't know about RH, but the installer should
> be aware of that.

An installer used by RH is aware of that, will leave a space for a
bootblock and will write one ... but if you are "recycling" a disk and
there are traces of an old partition table then an installer will NOT
change a partition type and, unfortunately, will be silent about it.  It
assumes that you know what you are doing. You can notice that something
is not right while asigning partitions to mount points but you have to
be aware of the issue.

In other words if the old partition table was of a FAT type then you
will end up with the same and no boot block.  It is still possible
to boot from another media (floppy, CD, another hard drive) and often
even rescue the whole thing without reinstalling from scratch but
if you have to ask how to do that then surely you do not want to go
that way and reinstalling will be faster. :-)  To avoid the problem
write few sectors of nulls (something like two should be enough but
you may want write few more) to a start of your disk before installing.
Can be done from a console which is a part of an installer.

Installing nowadays 7.1 instead of 7.2 is generally a mistake in more
ways than one.  At least apply immediately all available updates.

  Michal


